{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Then he sang.</b> There was no need to say ״then,״ but rather ״וַיָּשַׁר מֹשֶׁה״ and so on, and the matter is self-evident that then it occurred. However, the verse intends to inform us of the preparation of the apprehension, for when the fear of exaltedness and complete faith entered their hearts, then they merited to say song in the holy spirit." "And its saying <b>יָשִׁיר</b> is in the future tense, for since it said ״then״ the verse was concerned that a person might err and say that ״then״ implies a limitation, that this song cannot be said at any time other than then. Scripture therefore says ״יָשִׁיר״ — this song exists also for the future, and whoever comes to sing this song before HaShem, it is in El's hand to do so. Or it may hint at the mitzvah that it is a mitzvah to recite it always, and they fixed it in the morning prayer every day." "<b>And they said, saying: I will sing.</b> Meaning, they said to one another <b>לֵאמֹר</b> — that is, that they would say the song together, without any state of change or separation, until they would be as one man, although they were many. They intended together and did so, and they said <b>אָשִׁירָה</b> in the singular, as though they were one man — for otherwise they would have said נָשִׁירָה." "<b>For He is exalted with exaltation, etc.</b> One should not offer praise at the outset except for the downfall of the wicked, as it says (Proverbs 11:10) ״וּבַאֲבֹד רְשָׁעִים רִנָּה.״ And the meaning of its saying <b>גָאֹה גָּאָה</b> refers to Pharaoh, who became proud with a pride that is called pride, for the way of the proud is to be proud over one like themselves — a man over a man like himself — and the pride of this one is not counted as pride, for he exalts himself over the lowly. But Pharaoh — גָאֹה גָּאָה, meaning a pride that is truly called exalted pride, for he did not reckon with the word of the Most High and said ״מִי ה׳.״ Or it may intend <b>גָאֹה גָּאָה</b> that his pride was doubled: ״the horse and his rider — of the proud one — He hurled into the sea,״ etc." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend <b>גָאֹה גָּאָה</b> regarding ״YHWH reigns, He is clothed in pride״ (Psalms 93:1), and they intended to say that before He executed this judgment He had but a single degree of pride, and through the instrumentality of the great hand — גָאֹה גָּאָה. Also to allude, both among the supernal beings and among the lower beings, through the instrumentality of this matter. According to this reading, the noun modified by רוֹכְבוֹ is lacking. And perhaps the syntactic division of the verse is thus: ״אָשִׁירָה לַה׳ כִּי גָאֹה גָּאָה״ — meaning HaShem, and the phrase ״כִּי גָאֹה גָּאָה״ continues downward in this manner: and to Him who — גָאֹה גָּאָה — ״horse and, etc., He hurled into the sea.״"], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {4 "The phrase בָּעֶלְיוֹנִים בֵּין בַּתַּחְתּוֹנִים בֵּין likely means ״both among the supernal beings and among the lower beings,״ referring to the doubled pride manifest across all realms; translated hyper-literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:28:23.789066561Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}